# Queue Skeleton Requirements

## Scope
Minimal working queue for basket reservation requests to establish cover & move before any complexity.

## Out of Scope
Everything not in DoD (priority queues, idempotency, retry logic, circuit breaker, concurrency control, fingerprinting).

# Aftermath target
After completion: single Upstash Redis queue running 24/7, processing basket reservation requests one at a time with atomic operations, type-safe UI↔Redis↔CMS structure fidelity. All 6 DoD items verified with human-observable console logs.

## Definition of Done
[ ] Test suite: 1 E2E, 3 Integration, 1 Unit, 0 mocks, real infrastructure only, in /tests/checkout-queue-skeleton/<unit/integration/e2e> folder
    [ ] Test (Integration): Upstash connection
        [ ] Trace: Startup → connect to Upstash → verify connection → health check
        [ ] Setup: Upstash Redis Free Tier instance running
        [ ] Start conditions: UPSTASH_REDIS_URL environment variable set
        [ ] Assertion: Connection established, health check returns connected=true
        [ ] Justification: Verifies infrastructure foundation for all other tests
        [ ] Real feedback: Fails immediately if Redis unavailable, prevents false positives
    [ ] Test (Integration): Sequential FIFO processing
        [ ] Trace: 9 curl requests → queue processor → verify sequential logs
        [ ] Setup: Queue processor running, Redis connected
        [ ] Start conditions: Upstash connected, queue processor active
        [ ] Assertion: Logs show "Processing 1 → Complete 1 → Processing 2 → Complete 2 → ... → Processing 9 → Complete 9"
        [ ] Justification: Verifies core queue behavior prevents race conditions
        [ ] Real feedback: Detects parallel processing bugs, validates atomic operations
    [ ] Test (E2E): INPUT/OUTPUT flow
        [ ] Trace: 9 Playwright users → checkout button → UI request → Redis → Sanity CMS → response → UI
        [ ] Setup: Real browser, queue processor, Redis, Sanity CMS
        [ ] Start conditions: All infrastructure running, user authenticated
        [ ] Assertion: Logs show "Request received 1 → Sanity response 1 → Request received 2 → Sanity response 2 → ... → Request received 9 → Sanity response 9"
        [ ] Justification: Only test that verifies real user flow end-to-end
        [ ] Real feedback: Detects integration failures between UI, queue, and CMS
    [ ] Test (Integration): Type/structure fidelity
        [ ] Trace: Malformed request → structure validation → error
        [ ] Setup: Queue processor with type checking enabled
        [ ] Start conditions: Upstash connected, type validation active
        [ ] Assertion: Logs show 6 structure checks fail in order, error returned
        [ ] Justification: Prevents data corruption from malformed input
        [ ] Real feedback: Catches type mismatches before they corrupt queue/CMS
    [ ] Test (Unit): TypeScript type validation
        [ ] Trace: Malformed request → TypeScript type check → compile error
        [ ] Setup: TypeScript compiler
        [ ] Start conditions: Source code with type annotations
        [ ] Assertion: Compile fails with type error for malformed request
        [ ] Justification: Catches type errors at compile time, not runtime
        [ ] Real feedback: Prevents type-related bugs before deployment


[ ] Single production queue (no other instances), 0 cost, runs 24/7: **Upstash Redis (Free Tier) + Redis Streams**
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Upstash connection established', { url, status })` on startup
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Queue health check', { connected, latency })` every 60s

[ ] Sequential FIFO processing, atomic operations: One request at a time, atomic (wait 100% complete before starting next)
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Processing request', { requestId, queuePosition })` at start
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Request complete', { requestId, duration })` at end
    [ ] Test: `for i in {1..9}; do curl -X POST /api/checkout-queue & done; wait`, verify logs show exactly: "Processing request 1 → "Request complete 1" → "Processing request 2" → "Request complete 2" → ... → "Processing request 9" → "Request complete 9"

[ ] INPUT/OUTPUT flow: Basket reservation requests stream from ui → queued one at a time → atomic → stream to Sanity CMS → on response, pop currently processed from queue -> pass response back to UI
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Request received', { requestId, type })` on enqueue, then `console.log('TRACE: Sanity response', { requestId, success })` after CMS returns, verify logs show request received before Sanity response
    [ ] Test: Playwright test simulates 9 different users clicking checkout button simultaneously, verify console logs show exactly: "Request received 1" → "Sanity response 1" → "Request received 2" → "Sanity response 2" → ... → "Request received 9" → "Sanity response 9"

[ ] Type/structure fidelity: UI request structure → Redis queue structure → CMS request structure, CMS response structure → Redis queue structure → UI response structure
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: UI request structure exists', { structure: 'UIRequest', valid: true })` before sending to Redis
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Redis queue structure exists', { structure: 'RedisQueue', valid: true })` before sending to CMS
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: CMS request structure exists', { structure: 'CMSRequest', valid: true })` before processing
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: CMS response structure exists', { structure: 'CMSResponse', valid: true })` before returning
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Redis queue structure exists', { structure: 'RedisQueue', valid: true })` before returning to UI
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: UI response structure exists', { structure: 'UIResponse', valid: true })` before displaying
    [ ] Test: Send request, verify logs show 6 structure existence checks in order

[ ] TypeScript: Strict request/response type handling
    [ ] Verification: `console.log('TRACE: Type validation', { requestId, requestType, responseType })` on each request/response
    [ ] Test: Send malformed request, verify type validation error in logs
